🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/TensorIteratorInternal.h#L23
Hello, I've identified a potential optimization in the get_data_ptrs. The declaration is as follows:
inline void get_data_ptrs(
    char** ptrs,
    ArrayRef<char*> base,
    IntArrayRef strides,
    IntArrayRef counter) {
  const int64_t ntensors = base.size();
  const int64_t ndim = counter.size();
  std::copy(base.begin(), base.end(), ptrs);
  for (const auto dim : c10::irange(ndim)) {
    int64_t value = counter[dim];
    for (const auto arg : c10::irange(ntensors)) {
      ptrs[arg] += value * strides[dim * ntensors + arg];
    }
  }
}
This function is invoked with:
      c10::SmallBuffer<char*, 4> ptrs(ntensors);
      get_data_ptrs(ptrs.data(), {base_ptrs, ntensors}, strides, {range.begin});
Alternatives
Here, {base_ptrs, ntensors} is used for copying base_ptrs to ptrs. May we modify the function's signature and implementation, removing the ArrayRef argument for efficiency and readability? The revised function would be:
inline void get_data_ptrs(
    char** ptrs,
    size_t ntensors, // adding a new argument `ntensors` here
    IntArrayRef strides,
    IntArrayRef counter) {
  const int64_t ndim = counter.size();
  for (const auto dim : c10::irange(ndim)) {
    int64_t value = counter[dim];
    for (const auto arg : c10::irange(ntensors)) {
      ptrs[arg] += value * strides[dim * ntensors + arg];
    }
  }
}
With this change, the function call can be updated as follows:
      c10::SmallBuffer<char*, 4> ptrs(ntensors);
      std::copy(base_ptrs, base_ptrs+ntensors, ptrs.data());
      get_data_ptrs(ptrs.data(), ntensors, strides, {range.begin});
This modification allows for a more direct and clear handling of the ptrs, potentially improving the performance by reducing the overhead associated with the ArrayRef construction and usage.
